Ladybird now resolves the math generic family to an installed math font.
Run in Chrome 158, Firefox 151 and WebKit 26.5 on macOS, all three draw
“font-family: math” in a math font rather than in the family after it.
Each engine keeps its own list. Chrome’s default math_font_family is
STIX Two Math on macOS, Latin Modern Math on Linux and Cambria Math on
Windows (IDS_MATH_FONT_FAMILY); Firefox’s font.name-list.serif.x-math
starts Latin Modern Math, STIX Two Math, XITS Math, Cambria Math,
Libertinus Math, DejaVu Math TeX Gyre; and WebKit’s CSSFontSelector
tries the math font setting, then Latin Modern Math, STIX Two Math, XITS
Math, STIX Math, Libertinus Math and TeX Gyre fonts. Ladybird’s list
puts STIX Two Math first, as Chrome does on macOS, then takes the others
in the order Firefox and WebKit list them, ending with Cambria Math and
with STIXGeneral, which macOS ships. With no math font installed, it
falls back to the serif font, as Firefox’s list and WebKit’s do at their
ends; Chrome skips to the next family instead. The CI image has no math
font, and fontconfig there has no math alias. The report also named
emoji and fangsong, which stay family names: Chrome and WebKit serialize
them quoted or not without quotes, as they do any family name, and on
macOS no engine draws text any differently in either of them.
